---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "Mental health and excess mortality in Reading";
const description =
  "A Python analysis of public health data comparing Reading’s mortality gap with England and nearby areas.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="reading-case-study">
    <div class="case-study-wrap">
      <nav class="project-nav" aria-label="Project navigation">
        <a href="/projects/">← Back to Projects</a>
      </nav>

      <header class="case-study-hero">
        <div class="eyebrow">Case study · Public health analytics</div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="project-intro">In 2022–2024, the death rate among Reading residents aged 18–74 with a qualifying recorded referral to secondary mental health services was <strong>about 5.6 times</strong> the rate among those without that referral history. This equates to <strong>458.6% excess mortality</strong>, significantly above the England benchmark of <strong>378.5%</strong>.</p>
        <p>I used Python and public health data from Fingertips to explore how this gap changed over time and how Reading compared with neighbouring areas.</p>
      </header>
      <section class="case-study-section chart-sections">
        <div class="chart">
          <h2>How has the gap changed over time?</h2>
          <figure>
            <img src="/images/projects/reading-excess-mortality/mortality-trend.svg" width="612" height="373" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Line chart comparing excess mortality in Reading and England from 2015–2017 to 2022–2024, with a shaded confidence interval for Reading.">
            <figcaption>Reading’s estimate peaked at <strong>505.8% in 2020–2022</strong> and fell to <strong>458.6% by 2022–2024</strong>. Each point on the chart covers three years, so consecutive estimates share two years of data. It is too early to tell whether the gap is narrowing consistently.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <a class="notebook-link" href="https://jameslittlewood.net/notebooks/reading-chart1/">View the trend notebook and Python code</a>
        </div>
        <div class="chart">
          <h2>How does Reading compare with nearby areas?</h2>
          <figure>
            <img src="/images/projects/reading-excess-mortality/geographical-comparison.svg" width="721" height="372" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Dot-and-interval chart for 2022–2024. England 378.5%, South East 423.6%, Reading 458.6%, West Berkshire 471.5% and Wokingham 642.3%.">
            <figcaption>In 2022–2024, Reading’s estimate was higher than the <strong>South East figure of 423.6%</strong>. Both neighbouring authorities in Berkshire West recorded higher estimates than Reading. West Berkshire’s was <strong>471.5%</strong>, while Wokingham had the highest estimate of the areas shown at <strong>642.3%</strong>.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <a class="notebook-link" href="https://jameslittlewood.net/notebooks/reading-geo-comparison/reading_geo">View the area comparison notebook and Python code</a>
        </div>
      </section>
      <div class="case-study-section">
        <p><strong>Reading the charts.</strong> The shaded band on the trend chart and the horizontal lines on the area chart show 95% confidence intervals. Wider intervals indicate greater uncertainty around an estimate.</p>
      </div>
      <section class="case-study-section" aria-labelledby="conclusion">
        <h2 id="conclusion">What would I investigate next?</h2>
        <p>Reading’s mortality gap remains a public health concern. The latest estimate was lower than in the previous period, but the gap remained significantly wider than England’s. I’d follow future reporting periods to see whether the decline continues.</p>
        <p>I’d look for more detailed local data to see which age groups and neighbourhoods in Reading have the largest mortality gaps. For example, I’d explore whether the gap varies with income or deprivation, how it differs between ethnic groups, and how those patterns relate to access to healthcare.</p>
        <p>I’d also look more closely at who the referral records capture. Someone who receives mental health support solely through their GP or a voluntary group may fall into the comparison group if they have no qualifying recorded referral to secondary mental health services. Understanding who appears in each group would help me interpret the figures.</p>
        <p>Finally, I’d incorporate other Fingertips profiles into the analysis, such as <em>Adult Mental Health and Wellbeing</em> and <em>Suicide Prevention</em>. Looking at these alongside the mortality figures would give a fuller picture of local needs and help inform recommendations for Reading’s public health team and NHS partners.</p>
      </section>
      <footer class="project-footer"><em>Tools: Python, Matplotlib and Jupyter. Source: <a href="https://fingertips.phe.org.uk/profile/mortality-profile">Fingertips mortality data</a>, indicator 93582.</em></footer>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading-case-study {
    --case-line: color-mix(in oklab, var(--border) 50%, transparent);
    --case-tint: color-mix(in oklab, var(--muted) 20%, var(--card));
    --section-gap: 36px;
    --section-inset: 28px;
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    padding: 34px 26px 56px;
    background: var(--card);
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .reading-case-study::before {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    inset: 0 0 auto;
    height: 440px;
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 85% 90% at 100% 0, oklch(0.76 0.14 285 / 0.2), transparent 80%);
    content: "";
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global(.dark) .reading-case-study::before {
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .case-study-wrap {
    max-width: 750px;
    margin: auto;
  }

  .project-nav {
    margin-bottom: 43px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-weight: 650;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  a:hover,
  a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 12px 0 18px;
    font-size: clamp(31px, 5vw, 46px);
    line-height: 1.13;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 15px;
    font-size: 25px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  p,
  figcaption {
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-top: 16px;
  }

  p strong,
  figcaption strong {
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .project-intro {
    max-width: 650px;
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .case-study-section {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
    padding-top: var(--section-inset);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--case-line);
  }

  .chart-sections {
    margin-top: calc(var(--section-gap) / 2);
    padding-top: calc(var(--section-gap) / 2 - 1px);
  }

  .chart + .chart {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
  }

  .chart h2 {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--case-line);
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: white;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }

  .notebook-link {
    display: block;
    width: fit-content;
    margin-top: 10px;
    padding: 10px 15px;
    border: 1px solid var(--case-line);
    border-radius: 9px;
    background: var(--case-tint);
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .project-footer {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--case-line);
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .reading-case-study {
      --section-gap: 28px;
      --section-inset: 20px;
      padding: 25px 18px 40px;
    }

    .reading-case-study::before {
      height: 300px;
      opacity: 0.55;
    }

    .project-nav {
      margin-bottom: var(--section-gap);
    }
  }
</style>
